{#
  THE REPORT. A web page that happens to be printed.

  Reached at /actions/leadgenerator/report/view?entry=<id>, which is also how Playwright
  reaches it before calling page.pdf(). So what you see in a browser at A4 width is what a
  prospect gets, and designing it is a refresh rather than a re-render.

  IT READS AND NEVER CALCULATES. Every number here came out of report.json, which came out
  of lib/surface.mjs over a partition asserted to tile the page exactly. Adding anything up
  in this file would be a second implementation of a number somebody may argue with.

  THE ANNOTATED PAGE IS ONE IMAGE SHOWN THROUGH SEVERAL WINDOWS. CSS cannot break an image
  across printed pages — it overflows and is cut, or shrinks to nothing — and a homepage
  screenshot is thousands of pixels tall (jersey.com is 16,368). So each sheet holds a box
  with the image as its background, offset by one sheet's worth, which is the only way the
  block labels stay readable.

  WHAT MUST SURVIVE ANY REDESIGN: the caveats. A number this tool could not measure is
  reported as unmeasured, and a report that quietly drops that is not this tool's report.
#}
{% set pct = { } %}
{% macro pc(n) %}{% if n is null %}—{% else %}{{ (n * 100)|number_format(1) }}%{% endif %}{% endmacro %}
{% import _self as r %}

{# A4 at 96dpi less the margin — the box an image has to fit in. Kept in step with
   PAGE in tools/audit/lib/pdf.mjs; if one moves the other has to. #}
{% set sheet = { width: 704, height: 1033 } %}
{% set shotWidth = report.width ?? 1440 %}
{% set shotHeight = report.height ?? 0 %}
{% set scaled = shotHeight * (sheet.width / shotWidth) %}
{% set sheets = min(12, max(1, (scaled / sheet.height)|round(0, 'ceil'))) %}

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>{{ report.url }} — homepage audit</title>
    <style>
        /* THE SITE'S OWN TYPE, resolved through craft.vite.asset so the content hash in the
           built filename is never written down here — a rebuild changes it, and a
           hardcoded path would fail by silently falling back to a system font, which on a
           PDF nobody would notice until a prospect had one.

           Declared here rather than by pulling in the whole site stylesheet: this page has
           its own layout and does not want the site's, and a print document that inherits
           a nav's rules is a document fighting its own CSS. */
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonMono';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-leicht.woff2') }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 500;
            font-style: normal;
            /* `block`, not `swap`: this is printed, not read while it loads, and a swap
               means Chromium can print the fallback if the font lands a moment late. */
            font-display: block;
        }
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonMono';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-halbfett.woff2') }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 600;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: block;
        }

        /* Matches $g-font-secondary in build/scss/theme/_setup.scss. */
        :root { --r-mono: 'JonSonMono', ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, monospace; }

        @page { size: A4; margin: 45px; }
        * { box-sizing: border-box; }
        body { margin: 0; font: 500 11px/1.55 var(--r-mono); color: #111; }

        /* On screen, stand the sheets on a grey field so the printed page is visible while
           it is being designed. Neither rule survives into the PDF. */
        @media screen {
            body { background: #e9e9ec; padding: 24px; }
            .r-sheet { width: 794px; min-height: 1123px; padding: 45px; margin: 0 auto 24px;
                background: #fff; box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.2); }
        }
        @media print { .r-sheet { break-after: page; padding: 0; } .r-sheet:last-child { break-after: auto; } }

        h1 { font-size: 22px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 2px; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
        h2 { font-size: 12px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 8px; }
        .r-meta { color: #666; margin-bottom: 22px; }
        table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
        th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600; border-bottom: 1px solid #111; padding: 5px 0; }
        /* Mono is already tabular, but saying so stops a fallback ruining the column. */
        td { padding: 5px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee; }
        .r-n { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-facts { margin: 20px 0; color: #333; }
        .r-caveats { margin-top: 22px; padding: 12px; background: #f6f6f6; }
        .r-caveats li { margin-bottom: 6px; }

        /* One image, several windows onto it. `background-position` walks down it a sheet
           at a time; the box is a fixed height so a short last slice does not stretch. */
        .r-shot { width: {{ sheet.width }}px; height: {{ sheet.height }}px;
            background-image: url('{{ annotated }}');
            background-repeat: no-repeat;
            background-size: {{ sheet.width }}px auto; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<section class="r-sheet">
    <h1>{{ report.url }}</h1>
    <p class="r-meta">
        {{ report.width }}&times;{{ report.height }}px &middot;
        {{ report.blocks }} blocks &middot;
        {{ r.pc(report.coverage) }} of it drawn on &middot;
        measured {{ report.capturedAt|date('j F Y') }}
    </p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>&nbsp;</th>
                <th class="r-n">share of page</th>
                <th class="r-n">content to space</th>
                <th class="r-n">first viewport</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for c in report.categories %}
                <tr>
                    <td>{{ c.category }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.share) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.coverage) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.firstViewport) }}</td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="r-facts">
        {% if report.weightSummary %}<div>{{ report.weightSummary }}</div>{% endif %}
        {% if report.speed %}
            <div>PageSpeed {{ report.speed.score }}/100 desktop &middot;
                LCP {{ report.speed.lab.lcpMs }}ms &middot; TBT {{ report.speed.lab.tbtMs }}ms</div>
        {% endif %}
        {% if report.styles %}
            <div>{{ report.styles.colours.total }} colours &middot;
                {{ report.styles.fonts.loaded }} font faces loaded of {{ report.styles.fonts.declared }} declared &middot;
                {{ report.styles.fonts.rendered|length }} rendered</div>
        {% endif %}
    </div>

    {% if report.caveats|length %}
        <section class="r-caveats">
            <h2>What this does not cover</h2>
            <ul>
                {% for c in report.caveats %}
                    <li><b>{{ c.effect }}</b> {{ c.message }}</li>
                {% endfor %}
            </ul>
        </section>
    {% endif %}
</section>

{% for i in 0..(sheets - 1) %}
    <section class="r-sheet">
        <div class="r-shot" style="background-position: 0 -{{ i * sheet.height }}px;"></div>
    </section>
{% endfor %}

</body>
</html>
